[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Reentrancy and Access Control Flaws in Blockchain"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Smart contract security is paramount in blockchain development. Among the most critical vulnerabilities are reentrancy attacks and access control flaws. This tutorial explores the mechanics of these vulnerabilities and how to mitigate them effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Reentrancy Vulnerabilities"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A reentrancy attack occurs when a malicious contract calls back into the vulnerable contract before the first invocation is finished. This typically happens when state variables are updated after external calls are made."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// VULNERABLE CONTRACT EXAMPLE
contract VulnerableBank {
    mapping(address => uint) public balances;

    function withdraw() public {
        uint balance = balances[msg.sender];
        require(balance > 0, "Insufficient balance");

        // External call happens before state update
        (bool success, ) = msg.sender.call{value: balance}("");
        require(success, "Transfer failed");

        balances[msg.sender] = 0; // State updated too late
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigating Reentrancy"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern: Always update state variables before making external calls."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement a mutex lock (e.g., OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuard) to prevent recursive calls during execution."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// SECURED CONTRACT EXAMPLE
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/security/ReentrancyGuard.sol";

contract SecuredBank is ReentrancyGuard {
    mapping(address => uint) public balances;

    function withdraw() public nonReentrant {
        uint balance = balances[msg.sender];
        require(balance > 0, "Insufficient balance");

        balances[msg.sender] = 0; // State updated first

        (bool success, ) = msg.sender.call{value: balance}("");
        require(success, "Transfer failed");
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Access Control Flaws"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Access control flaws occur when sensitive functions lack proper modifiers to restrict who can execute them. If an administrative function does not verify msg.sender, any user can call it and potentially compromise the entire contract."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// VULNERABLE ACCESS CONTROL EXAMPLE
contract VulnerableOwner {
    address public owner;

    // Missing constructor, anyone can claim ownership!
    function setOwner(address newOwner) public {
        owner = newOwner;
    }

    function sensitiveFunction() public {
        // Perform critical logic
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigating Access Control Flaws"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always initialize critical state variables like ownership inside the constructor function."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use robust access control libraries like OpenZeppelin's Ownable or AccessControl."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// SECURED ACCESS CONTROL EXAMPLE
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol";

contract SecuredOwner is Ownable {
    constructor() Ownable(msg.sender) {}

    function sensitiveFunction() public onlyOwner {
        // Only the owner can execute this logic
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing smart contracts requires strict adherence to established design patterns. By protecting against reentrancy with proper ordering and mutexes, and enforcing strict access control using libraries like OpenZeppelin, developers can significantly reduce the risk of catastrophic exploits."}]
